MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Each of these ran a lifecycle .py/.sh that scan_gateway_lifecycle never opened ((False, None) on
fb2398dd9bf, found in the review of PR #2393):

1. A descriptor duplication on the feeding command: `cat f.py 2>&1 | python3`,
   `{ cat f.py; } 2>&1 | python3`, `{ cat f.py; } >&2 | python3`, `(cat f.py) 2>&1 | python3`.
   _shlex_tokens splits on `&`, so `2>&1` became `2>`, `&`, `1`; the walk read the `&` as the
   background operator and the feed became cat_operands(['1']) = [].
2. Output process substitution: `cat f.py > >(python3)`. The `>(…)` body read nothing.
3. A `cat` with no file operand passing its stdin on: `cat f.py | cat | python3`,
   `cat f.py | if true; then cat; fi | python3` (`tee` too).
4. A pipe continued on the next line: `cat f.py |` newline `python3`. Each line was walked alone.

Fix (the walk is in cron/lifecycle_guard_groups.py; lifecycle_guard.py stays under its cap):

- protect_redirection_ampersands swaps the `&` of an unquoted, unescaped `>&`, `<&`, `&>`, `&>>`
  for a private-use word character before shlex, and _shlex_tokens restores it per token, so a
  redirection stays one word. `&&`, `|&` and a lone `&` keep their meaning. _REDIRECTION learns
  `&>`/`&>>`, so `python3 &>log` still reads its program from the pipe.
- A closed `( … )` keeps its output across the redirection words after it, like `}`/`fi` already
  did; the next operator ends it.
- `>(…)` feeds its body what its command writes so far; the outer command's own feed is restored
  when the substitution closes.
- Bare `cat` reuses main's _cat_relays_stdin (#2586), which now also covers `tee` (its operands
  are files it writes); _LineWalk.writes adds the feed for such a segment, both when a segment
  flushes and when `>(…)` takes what its command writes.
- The two cron.lifecycle_guard_groups imports in lifecycle_guard.py are folded into one.
- join_continued_lines joins a line ending in `|`, `|&`, `&&` or `||` with the next one before the
  walk (comments are already dropped by shlex, so `| # note` continues too).

Still allowed: `cat f.py &>/dev/null | python3 c.py`, `cat f.py 2>&1 | wc -l`,
`cat f.py > out; python3 c.py`, `cat f.py 2>&1 & python3`, `cat clean | tee f.py | python3`.

Proof:
- New tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_feed_routing.py (49 cases). On the base 28 fail: 27 of the
  34 must-scan cases plus the tokenizer contract; the other 7 must-scan cases are regression
  guards. Every must-allow case passes on base and head.
- 22 mutants of the added conditions: all killed (one equivalent clause removed).
- Every test file importing the guard, terminal_tool_guards, approval_detection, shell_heredoc
  or code_execution_tool: 69 files, 2995 passed, 0 failed.
- Replay of 22,531 distinct real terminal commands from a worker's state.db through
  scan_gateway_lifecycle, base vs head: 0 changed verdicts, 0 errors (17 blocked on both).
- scripts/check --only health: 0 blocking.

Not covered here: a compound command spread over several lines (`{` newline `cat f.py` newline
`} | python3`, `| while …; do` newline `python3` newline `done`) is still walked line by line.
